"""
Real-time pulse extraction using the POS algorithm.

pos_algorithm(), bandpass_filter(), estimate_heart_rate(), calculate_snr(),
mean_skin_rgb(), landmarks_to_pixels(), build_face_landmarker(), and
ensure_face_landmarker_model() are copied VERBATIM and never touched by
any of the tracker/diagnostics work below. compute_roi_rects(),
_mask_from_rects(), and build_roi_mask() (the four-patch physiological
ROI: forehead/left cheek/right cheek/chin) are also unchanged.

# ===== TRACKING ARCHITECTURE CHANGE: KLT + ECC -> ORB + RANSAC =====
KLT (cv2.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK / cv2.goodFeaturesToTrack) and ECC
(cv2.findTransformECC) have been removed completely. Motion tracking is
now:

    MediaPipe init -> physiological ROI definition -> ORB feature
    detection (expanded search region around the ROI, never the whole
    frame) -> ORB descriptor matching (previous frame vs current frame)
    -> RANSAC outlier rejection (cv2.estimateAffinePartial2D) -> dominant
    motion from RANSAC inliers -> normalized_motion = median inlier
    displacement / ROI diagonal -> motion-threshold decision -> ...

  - MediaPipe runs only for the INITIAL detection and for genuine
    RECOVERY: too few ORB keypoints/matches, RANSAC failing to fit a
    model or having too few inliers, or the resulting tracked position
    leaving the frame. It is never triggered merely because
    normalized_motion is above the threshold.
  - ORB (cv2.ORB_create) detects+describes keypoints inside a rectangle
    expanded around the last known tracked position (ORB_SEARCH_PADDING_RATIO),
    never the whole frame/face -- this is a MOTION-ESTIMATION region only,
    entirely separate from the four physiological ROI patches that
    mean_skin_rgb() reads pixels from. ORB features never select rPPG
    pixels.
  - Matches (cv2.BFMatcher, NORM_HAMMING) between the previous frame's
    ORB descriptors and the current frame's feed cv2.estimateAffinePartial2D
    with RANSAC, which both fits a similarity transform (translation +
    rotation + uniform scale) AND flags inlier/outlier matches -- outliers
    are discarded before any motion is measured, exactly as required.
  - The dominant-motion metric used for the threshold decision is the
    MEDIAN of the individual RANSAC-inlier displacement magnitudes (not
    the median of a vector's components) normalized by the ROI diagonal
    (sqrt(w^2+h^2) of the union of the four ROI patches, fixed at the
    last MediaPipe (re)detection until the next one):
        normalized_motion = median(||curr_inlier - prev_inlier||) / roi_diagonal

# ===== UPDATE: ROI-FOLLOWING -> FIXED-ROI FRAME ALIGNMENT =====
# The paragraph below this one (kept for history) describes the
# PREVIOUS behavior -- ROI coordinates updated algebraically, RGB frame
# never warped. That behavior has been REPLACED. The four physiological
# ROI patches (forehead / left cheek / right cheek / chin) no longer
# move.
#
# On the first successful MediaPipe detection, and on every genuine
# MediaPipe recovery, the ROI rectangles are frozen as `reference_rois`
# and the frame at that moment becomes the implicit reference coordinate
# system.
#
# ORB + RANSAC still estimate frame-to-frame motion exactly as before
# (previous frame's keypoints vs current frame's keypoints -> RANSAC
# similarity transform, previous -> current). That per-frame transform
# is additionally composed into a running current-frame -> reference-
# frame transform (`to_reference_transform`), using the mathematically
# correct INVERSE of each per-frame RANSAC transform (RANSAC estimates
# previous -> current; alignment needs current -> reference, i.e. the
# inverse chained back through every prior frame since the reference was
# established).
#
#   normalized_motion <= MOTION_THRESHOLD  (SMALL motion)
#       -> no warp; the fixed reference ROI is read directly from the
#          raw current frame (misalignment at this scale is treated as
#          negligible).
#   normalized_motion >  MOTION_THRESHOLD  (LARGE motion), and the
#   RANSAC estimate passes the existing reliability checks
#       -> cv2.warpAffine(current_frame, to_reference_transform,
#          flags=cv2.INTER_NEAREST) aligns the CURRENT RGB frame into
#          the reference coordinate system; the fixed reference ROI is
#          then read from that aligned frame.
#
# Either way, mean_skin_rgb() always reads pixels at the ROI's ORIGINAL,
# unmoving reference coordinates, from a frame that is either already
# the reference frame or has been warped into the reference frame's
# coordinate system. `last_roi_rects` / `last_roi_bbox` inside
# FaceROITracker still move frame-to-frame, but ONLY to tell ORB where
# to search for keypoints next frame -- they are no longer used to
# decide which pixels feed mean_skin_rgb().
# =============================================================================

  - normalized_motion <= MOTION_THRESHOLD -> [PREVIOUS BEHAVIOR, REPLACED
    ABOVE] accept the RANSAC-estimated translation+scale directly and
    update the ROI coordinates.
  - normalized_motion > MOTION_THRESHOLD -> the same transform is only
    accepted if RANSAC found enough inliers to trust it at this larger
    scale (MIN_RANSAC_INLIERS_LARGE_MOTION, stricter than the normal-
    motion minimum) AND the resulting tracked position stays in bounds;
    otherwise this triggers MediaPipe recovery. This is the "extra
    validation for large motion" -- RANSAC's own inlier count is the
    confidence signal, no separate mechanism is introduced.
  - [PREVIOUS BEHAVIOR, REPLACED ABOVE] ROI coordinates were updated
    algebraically (translate + scale the rectangle) -- the RGB frame
    itself was never warped, remapped, or interpolated. This has been
    replaced by fixed-ROI frame alignment as described above.

Whichever mechanism supplied the current frame's aligned/raw frame
(MediaPipe or ORB), the actual rPPG pixel values are always read fresh
from that frame at the fixed reference ROI location -- never from a
ROI rectangle that followed the face.

# ===== PERFORMANCE DIAGNOSTICS =====
The 10-second-window summary keeps the original format and reports:
MediaPipe/ORB frames & usage %, MediaPipe re-detections, ORB failures,
RANSAC failures, valid ORB matches, RANSAC inliers, median displacement,
normalized motion, motion classification (SMALL/LARGE), actual FPS, HR,
SNR, and now alignment_applied (frames where the current frame was
actually warped back into the reference coordinate system). All old
KLT/ECC-specific statistics have been removed.

Requires: pip install mediapipe opencv-python scipy numpy
(ORB and estimateAffinePartial2D both ship in core opencv-python --
no opencv-contrib-python required.)
"""

